    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Yamaha Previews the Future of the Tenere Adventure Bikes With Mysterious 4 Concept\nB-21 Raider Closer to Becoming the World's First Sixth-Gen Bomber After Weapons Release Test\n19 photos\nPhoto: Northrop Grumman\nQuickly and carefully, the United States is moving closer and closer to becoming the first nation on the planet to operate a sixth-generation aircraft. That would be the B-21 Raider nuclear bomber presently being developed by defense contractor Northrop Grumman for the U.S. Air Force (USAF).\nJust four years after it was first presented at the company's facilities in Palmdale, California, the stealth nuclear bomber reached another important development milestone after executing its first-ever weapons release test. In other words, it proved it can drop bombs, which will be its main mission once it becomes operational.\nThe test was announced by Northrop Grumman earlier this month, but without any details on what was actually done or what the objectives were. We're only told the test showed the B-21’s ability to carry and employ combat power.\nWith this out of the way, the defense contractor is now accelerating the development of the plane, as it still plans to deliver the first operational example to the Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota as soon as next year.\nAs per the limited details on the design available at the time of writing, the Raider has been imagined so that it can deploy a variety of weapons, from nuclear and conventional bombs to missiles of a wide variety.\nUp to 20,000 pounds (9,100 kg) of munitions can be transported at a given time, and the design is made in such a way as to allow the accommodation of weapons that have not yet been developed.\nThe announcement concerning the weapons release test comes about half a year after we were told the Raider had successfully performed aerial refueling tests, something that truly unlocks its capability of reaching any point on the planet without having to land.\nIn the meantime, we were also informed that the plane had demonstrated outstanding stealth performance, but no details on what that means have been provided. Lifetimes of fatigue testing have also been completed, months ahead of schedule.\nWhen fully operational, the Raider will eventually replace two of the three airplanes that currently form the trio of nuclear-capable bombers, the B-1B Lancer (introduced in 1986) and the B-2 Spirit, which has been in the sky since 1997. The third plane, the B-52 Stratofortress, will fly alongside the Raider into the 2050s, effectively becoming the world's first aircraft to still be operational a century after its first flight.\nWhen it comes to capabilities, the information released so far paints the picture of a potent machine. Powered by a pair of Pratt & Whitney PW9000 non-afterburning turbofan engines rated at 27,000 pounds of thrust each, the bomber will be capable of flying at subsonic speeds of Mach 0.8 (614 mph/988 kph), and will fly at max altitudes of 50,000 feet (15,000 meters).\nThe aircraft will first be deployed at the Ellsworth Air Force Base, which will also be the place where Raider pilots will train. Later down the line, airbases Whiteman in Missouri and Dyess in Texas will accommodate it as well. There is word of the USAF needing a fleet of up to 100 of these bombers.\nThe designation of the stealth plane follows the USAF tradition, with B designating a bomber, and the number 21 the century the plane was born. The word Raider references Lt. Col. James 'Jimmy' Doolittle and his crew of 80 airmen who flew 16 B-25 Mitchell medium bombers on a raid on Tokyo and other cities on the island of Honshu in the wake of the Pearl Harbor attack.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nb-21 raider Northrop Grumman nuclear bomber\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT All 2026 Formula 1 Drivers Can Now Be Had as LEGO Minifigures for the First Time, Race Cars in Tow",
